---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, href } from '../lib/site';

/*
 * SOURCE TRACE (Site build 2, Perthos/Research#138)
 *
 * Body prose: `notes/career-narrative-draft.md` §2, the canonical 604-word
 * narrative, carried near-verbatim. This is the one surface with room for it,
 * so it runs at full length rather than in the compressed form the short
 * surfaces take.
 *
 * Three source-traced amendments to §2, each with its own ruling:
 *   - "a technology org of 200-plus" inserted into the chronology, per the
 *     round-6 scale ruling (`notes/linkedin-rewrite-draft.md` §15: 200-plus
 *     everywhere in shipping prose, "I don't want to overstate").
 *   - Layoff telling is Variant A, the full ruled paragraph
 *     (`career-narrative-draft.md` §3). #138 licenses Variant A for this
 *     surface specifically.
 *   - The unfinished-charge paragraph is added from
 *     `notes/linkedin-rewrite-draft.md` §21, which routes it here by name.
 *     COMFORT FLAG: it states publicly that the charge was not completed.
 *     Only the operator can authorize that disclosure. Cutting it removes one
 *     paragraph and nothing else depends on it.
 *
 * Deliberately absent: "servant leadership" as a claim anywhere; any AI-skill
 * claim; any growyouthgiving line (`linkedin-rewrite-draft.md` §6 rules the
 * strand gets its own placement, not an orphan sentence in a professional
 * summary, and the open question about its next move must not be pre-decided
 * here). The strand is in the footer.
 *
 * Voice: zero em-dashes throughout.
 */
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description={SITE.description}
  ogSlug="about"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Engineering executive and practicing architect. I work out what changes
      in a software organization when AI becomes part of the system, and I
      show the evidence.
    </p>
    <hr class="page-head__rule" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      If you run an engineering organization right now, you are being asked to
      make decisions about AI faster than anyone can hand you evidence for
      them. Ship it, don't ship it, restructure the team around it, tell your
      board what it's worth. That's the problem I work on, and I don't have a
      tidy answer to sell you.
    </p>

    <p>
      I'm an engineering executive and a practicing enterprise architect.
      Those are usually two people. Doing both is why the questions I care
      about sit across the line. How work should decompose between humans,
      models, and the systems that check them is a technical architecture
      question and an organizational one at the same time, and you can't
      answer half of it and call it done.
    </p>

    <p>
      The work started inside a company, not as a writing project. More than
      half my engineers weren't yet fluent enough in these systems to design
      around them responsibly, and I wasn't willing to ask them to build on
      something whose failure modes I couldn't explain. So I went looking for
      what we actually know. Most of what I found was a small number of claims
      echoing loudly, so I ended up building a research process before I had
      anything to say. Commissioned questions, the framing audited for bias
      before acquisition starts, an independent pass whose whole job is to
      attack the result. The first thing it caught was me, commissioning my
      own conclusion. That's the reason I trust it at all, and it's still an
      instrument, not an authority. What it produces, and the rules it is held
      to, are on <a href={href('/standards')}>Standards</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Getting here wasn't a straight line. I started in electrical engineering
      at Micron, on flash reliability, and my first boss out of school gave me
      the thing I still run on: trust your process, verify the output. Eight
      years at Tableau took me from tier four support to software engineering,
      then product, then program management, then back into architecture at
      the director level, until the January 2023 layoffs ended it. I took the
      summer off and started at Comscore that October, running architecture
      and a group of about thirty. Our CTO left the following June and I
      picked up technical strategy for a technology org of 200-plus. In
      September 2025 I took the SVP promotion and an engineering organization
      of about ninety.
    </p>

    <p>
      Late this July I wrote a field guide for the architects I lead. They'd
      noticed I kept deploying the same eighteen sayings and asked what was
      actually underneath them, so I wrote the reasoning down, with the prior
      art collected in an appendix because standing on somebody's shoulders is
      a feature. The stated goal of the document was that they could
      eventually operate the way I do without me in the room. That's the job
      as I understand it. A system that works in spite of me, not because of
      me. It is on this site, unchanged, as the{' '}
      <a href={href('/field-guide')}>Field Guide</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two weeks later I was laid off. That's twice in under four years,
      Tableau in the post-COVID downsizing and Comscore on August eleventh.
      Neither one was about the work, and neither one is a story about a
      company. Headcount corrections have been running through this industry
      for four years, and this time I was in one.
    </p>

    <p>
      My last charge was the course from a legacy data estate to an
      architecture that could carry the business into its next decade. I
      believed a data mesh could do it if we solved the governance problem. We
      solved neither before my tenure ended. The diagnosis is still untested,
      and that stings. It is also the reason this work exists in the shape it
      does: I got to observe and teach, and I did not get to enact and
      measure.
    </p>

    <p>
      So that's what I'm doing with the time. The internal work, made public:
      the question, what the evidence supports, what it doesn't cover, and the
      decision I'd make anyway. Every number and every attribution carries a
      named source and a live link, or it comes out of the piece. I'd rather
      publish nothing than add to the noise.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you're making these calls too, I want to hear where your experience
      doesn't match mine. Feedback is a gift and that's exactly the kind I'm
      looking for. The pieces are on <a href={href('/writing')}>Writing</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
